All six claims verified. Report below.

---

## 1. GameRenderer remesh — **TRUE**

`/Users/hutusi/workspace/ai/david/monecraft/lib/game/render/GameRenderer.ts:616-637`:

```ts
  private syncWorldMesh(state: GameState): void {
    const regionX = Math.floor(state.player.position.x / RENDER_GRID) * RENDER_GRID;
    const regionZ = Math.floor(state.player.position.z / RENDER_GRID) * RENDER_GRID;
    if (!state.worldMeshDirty && regionX === this.currentRegionX && regionZ === this.currentRegionZ) return;

    state.worldMeshDirty = false;
    this.currentRegionX = regionX;
    this.currentRegionZ = regionZ;

    this.rebuildWorldMesh(state.world, regionX, regionZ);
  }

  private rebuildWorldMesh(world: VoxelWorld, regionX: number, regionZ: number): void {
    const geometry = buildGeometryLayersRegion(world, regionX - RENDER_RADIUS, regionX + RENDER_RADIUS, regionZ - RENDER_RADIUS, regionZ + RENDER_RADIUS);
    this.scene.remove(this.worldMesh);
    ...
```

**Dimensions confirmed as 181×150×181.** `RENDER_RADIUS = 90`, `RENDER_GRID = 20` (`/Users/hutusi/workspace/ai/david/monecraft/lib/game/config.ts:433-434`) → X/Z span `regionX-90 … regionX+90` inclusive = 181. Y is unbounded by the call: `buildGeometryLayersRegion` defaults `minY = 0, maxY = world.sizeY - 1` (`/Users/hutusi/workspace/ai/david/monecraft/lib/world/meshing.ts:122-131`), and `WORLD_SIZE_Y = 150` (`/Users/hutusi/workspace/ai/david/monecraft/lib/world/blocks.ts:2`). One block edit anywhere → full 181×150×181 re-scan, new `BufferGeometry`, dispose + re-add both meshes.

**No chunking whatsoever.** No per-chunk mesh, no dirty-chunk set, no chunk manager. Grep for `chunk` in `lib/world/`, `lib/game/render/`, `lib/game/engine/state.ts` returns only two unrelated hits: a comment about "pale cobble chunks" in the texture atlas (`lib/world/atlas.ts:115`) and Three.js `ShaderChunk` (`GameRenderer.ts:46`). The whole dirty mechanism is one global boolean: `worldMeshDirty: boolean` (`/Users/hutusi/workspace/ai/david/monecraft/lib/game/engine/state.ts:420`). It is set (never per-region) in ~25 places — `interact.ts` (9×), `randomTicks.ts`, `redstone.ts`, `mining.ts`, `portal.ts`, `explosion.ts`, `treeGrowth.ts`, `GameEngine.ts:453/1372/1793/1816`, `NetworkSession.ts:439/624/918/932`.

**The renderer does clear engine-owned state**, and ordering is load-bearing. `state.worldMeshDirty = false` at `GameRenderer.ts:621` mutates the engine's `GameState`. Consumer order is enforced only by a comment in the rAF loop (`/Users/hutusi/workspace/ai/david/monecraft/lib/game/useMinecraftGame.ts:560-562`):

```ts
      // The minimap must read worldMeshDirty before renderer.sync clears it.
      minimap?.sync(gameEngine.state, now);
      renderer.sync(gameEngine.state, now);
```

This is documented as an invariant in `/Users/hutusi/workspace/ai/david/monecraft/docs/architecture.md:22` and `:196` — including a note that lighting was deliberately wired into the engine rather than the renderer "so the minimap-before-renderer `worldMeshDirty` ordering stays intact." So the coupling is known and load-bearing, but nothing but a comment protects it.

## 2. Minimap shares the flag — **TRUE**

`/Users/hutusi/workspace/ai/david/monecraft/lib/game/render/minimap.ts:79`:

```ts
      if (!baseBuilt || originX !== baseOriginX || originZ !== baseOriginZ || state.worldMeshDirty) {
        rebuildBase(state, originX, originZ);
      }
```

Read-only consumer of the same global flag, documented at `minimap.ts:4-9`. Rebuilds a full 128×128 column re-sample (`SAMPLE_SIZE = 128`, `minimap.ts:11`) at up to 10 Hz (`SYNC_INTERVAL_MS = 100`, `minimap.ts:13`). Any block edit anywhere in the world triggers both the 181×150×181 remesh and the 128×128 minimap re-sample.

## 3. No Web Workers — **TRUE** (all main-thread)

`grep -rn "new Worker\|OffscreenCanvas\|worker_threads"` across all `.ts`/`.tsx`/`.js` (excluding `node_modules`/`.next`) returns **zero hits**. No `SharedArrayBuffer` either. The only "worker" in the repo is a **service worker** (`/Users/hutusi/workspace/ai/david/monecraft/public/sw.js` — offline app-shell cache, `install`/`activate`/`fetch` handlers at lines 194/199/211) and Playwright's test workers. Worldgen (`lib/world/generation.ts`), lighting (`lib/world/lighting.ts` — `computeFullLight` BFS over the whole world), and meshing (`lib/world/meshing.ts`) all run synchronously on the browser main thread.

## 4. `docs/save-format.md` Math.sin — **TRUE** (stale line contradicted by the file itself and by the code)

The doc **contradicts itself**. `/Users/hutusi/workspace/ai/david/monecraft/docs/save-format.md:58` correctly records the migration away:

> "**portable worldgen noise**. Every seed-determined noise call … moved from `Math.sin` — whose results are engine-defined … to the bit-portable implementations in `lib/world/noise.ts`."

But `/Users/hutusi/workspace/ai/david/monecraft/docs/save-format.md:113` still says the opposite:

> "Caveat: the noise functions use `Math.sin`, whose exact results are engine-defined — the tests prove refactor purity on the pinned Bun version, not cross-browser save portability."

The portable implementation exists at `/Users/hutusi/workspace/ai/david/monecraft/lib/world/noise.ts` (lines 3, 16, 26-35 — a degree-11 Taylor sin range-reduced to a quarter wave via `Math.floor`). Line 113's caveat is stale and materially misleading: it denies cross-browser save portability that line 58 says was achieved and that `e2e/determinism.e2e.ts` proves (Chromium digest ≡ Bun baseline).

## 5. "client-only" — **TRUE** for AGENTS.md, **FALSE** for README/package.json

`/Users/hutusi/workspace/ai/david/monecraft/AGENTS.md:7` — stale on two counts:

> "Single-player, browser-based, client-only, saves to localStorage."

There is an authoritative server (`server/` — `index.ts`, `room.ts`, `roomRegistry.ts`, `persistence.ts`, `Dockerfile`, `fly.toml`), a Postgres database (`db/` — `schema.ts`, `migrations/`, drizzle), auth (`better-auth`, `lib/auth/`), and networking (`lib/net/NetworkSession.ts`). Saves also moved to IndexedDB, not localStorage. Since AGENTS.md is declared "the single source of truth" (line 3) and CLAUDE.md imports it, this is the description every agent reads first.

README is **not** stale — `/Users/hutusi/workspace/ai/david/monecraft/README.md:8` scopes the claim correctly ("Single-player is client-only … worlds in IndexedDB") and then describes online co-op. `package.json` has **no** `description` field at all (name is still `"minecraft-clone"`, `package.json:2`). `docs/architecture.md:207` uses "Client-only" as an SSR-hygiene rule, which is fine in context; `docs/deploy.md:4` says "it runs entirely in the browser" — that section is about the Vercel app, but the phrasing is loose.

## 6. Bun version — **TRUE, unpinned locally; CI pins 1.3.12**

- `package.json`: **no** `packageManager` field, **no** `engines` field (full file read — `/Users/hutusi/workspace/ai/david/monecraft/package.json`).
- **No** `.tool-versions`, **no** `.bun-version`, **no** `.nvmrc` in the repo root.
- CI pins it in two separate jobs: `/Users/hutusi/workspace/ai/david/monecraft/.github/workflows/ci.yml:18-20` and `:39-41` — `oven-sh/setup-bun@v2` with `bun-version: "1.3.12"`.
- The game server pins it a third time: `/Users/hutusi/workspace/ai/david/monecraft/server/Dockerfile:4` — `FROM oven/bun:1.3.12`.

So `1.3.12` is duplicated across three places with no single source of truth, and a local `bun install`/`bun test` uses whatever Bun the developer happens to have. This matters more than usual here: `docs/save-format.md:113` explicitly ties worldgen digest tests to "the pinned Bun version", and `lib/world/generationBaselines.ts` holds SHA-256 baselines that a Bun version drift could flip.

